\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Sign and clock edge weights}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Give the real line its usual inner product and let signs act by
multiplication. The orbit of #{1} is #{\{1,-1\}}. Take the site factors
equal to #{1} and give each edge the factor
#{b_e(u,v)=\ue^{w_e\langle u,v\rangle}}. For #{w_e>0} agreement
has weight #{\ue^{w_e}} and disagreement has weight #{\ue^{-w_e}}.
Flipping both endpoints preserves the factor.}

\p{For the clock with #{N\ge2} positions use the Euclidean plane,
with #{C_N} generated by rotation through #{2\pi /N}. Starting from
a unit vector gives a regular orbit of #{N} points. Index them by
#{j\in\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z} in rotation order. The inner product at
positions #{j,\ell} is #{\cos(2\pi(j-\ell)/N)}. Thus in the three-state
clock an edge pays weight #{\ue^{w_e}} for equal positions and
#{\ue^{-w_e/2}} for unequal positions. Both models constrain the scalar
to one radius. The real scalar of \ref{fdip-00GJ}
can vary in magnitude.}
